  "query_preview": "..(Saturday through Thursday)\nIssued at 318 PM EDT THU SEP 10 2026\n\nModel suites are in reasonable agreement for much of the long term \nperiod. The beginning of the period will feature a weaker upper level \nshortwave that will progress across the Ohio Valley. This weaker \nwave will keep shower and thunderstorm chances going in the first \npart of the weekend. Given the weak shear and increased PWAT values \nin the 1.75 to nearly 2 inch range, we could see a renewed threat of \nheavy rain given the increasing moisture and slow storm motions. \nGiven this, WPC does keep us in a isolated risk of flash flooding on \nSaturday. Then by Sunday the previous wave dampens as it progresses \nout of the area. The lower Ohio Valley will then see heights begin \nto rise in the wake. This will lead to isolated convection Sunday, \nmainly confined to the higher terrain given the lack of synoptic \nscale forcing.\n\nAs we move into the beginning of the work week and into mid-week, we \nwill see upper-level ridging become further established across the \nSoutheast US. This ridging will be quite strong for this time of \nyear, running above the 90th percentile. This will usher in mostly \ndry weather Monday into Tuesday, but with that will come warmer \ntemperatures. The warmest day right now is expected to be Tuesday, \nwith highs topping out in the upper 80s to lower 90s. Then by \nWednesday into Thursday this ridging will start to breakdown and a \ncold front will progress across the Ohio Valley. There has been some \ntiming differences with this boundary within the various model \nsuites that will need to be resolved, but overall expect increasing \nchances of showers and Thunderstorms by late next week. Than Some of \nthe guidance shows this boundary could usher in some much dryer and \ncooler weather by late in the week into next weekend.",
